# Lore's user-level ignore rules — a starting point, installed by
# `lore setup ignore` and never rewritten afterwards. This file is yours.
#
# Lore evaluates exactly three rule sources, lowest to highest (D-0020):
#
#   1. this file, which applies to every project you index;
#   2. each repository's own .gitignore, honoured as a courtesy;
#   3. each project's own .loreignore, which is sovereign.
#
# So a repo's .gitignore overrides a line here, and a project's .loreignore
# overrides both — `!.env` in a committed .loreignore really does index that
# file. Nothing below is compiled into lore: delete a line and lore stops
# applying it everywhere.
#
# Syntax is gitignore's. Patterns are unanchored, so `Library/` matches at any
# depth in any project; a leading `/` anchors to a project's root; a trailing
# `/` matches directories only; `!` re-includes; and later lines win.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dot-files and dot-directories
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mostly tool state rather than authored content. This one rule is the widest
# thing in the file — it also covers .venv/, .idea/, .vs/ and .obsidian/ — so
# it is the one most worth re-including from: a project that keeps its CI
# workflows worth searching writes `!.github/` in its own .loreignore.
.*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A floor, not a promise: patterns only, knowingly incomplete, and no entropy
# scanning (it is false-positive-prone and trains the reflex of switching the
# guard off). A private key that becomes a search result is a private key an
# agent can quote into a transcript, which is what these lines are for. The
# substantive protection is an encrypted store, not this list.
#
# Process environment files. `.env.local` and `.env.production` are the same
# file with a suffix, and that is where deployed secrets actually live.
.env
.env.*
# OpenSSH private keys, as `ssh-keygen` names them. The `.pub` half is public,
# but excluding it too costs nothing anybody wanted indexed.
id_rsa*
id_ecdsa*
id_ed25519*
id_dsa*
# PEM containers and generic key files. Broad on purpose: `*.key` also catches
# Keynote documents and some engine asset formats, and `*.pem` catches public
# certificate chains — the file name does not distinguish them, and a missed
# key is unrecoverable while a missed document is one `!` line away.
*.pem
*.key
# Credential directories, at any depth: a vendored `home/.ssh/` holds as real a
# private key as a root-level one. (`**/` because a pattern containing a slash
# would otherwise be anchored to the project root.)
.ssh/
.aws/
.gnupg/
**/.config/gcloud/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build output and vendored trees
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Machine-written, or written by somebody else — either way it drowns real hits
# and costs embedding time. `[Xx]` classes because Unity and MSBuild both vary
# the case of their own output directories, and matching is case-sensitive.
#
# Watch the two that can hide authored work: some ecosystems commit scripts to
# `bin/` (`bin/rails`, an npm package's `bin/`) and some commit a built `dist/`.
# A project in that position re-includes them (`![Bb]in/`).

# Rust
[Tt]arget/

# Node
node_modules/
dist/

# Python
__pycache__/
venv/
*.pyc

# .NET / MSBuild
[Bb]in/
[Oo]bj/

# Unity
[Ll]ibrary/
[Tt]emp/
[Ll]ogs/
[Bb]uild/
[Bb]uilds/
[Uu]serSettings/
[Mm]emoryCaptures/
*.meta
